{
  "schemaVersion": 2,
  "notes": [
    "The Synty building kit placed from the Docks tile map -- read by render3d/static_pieces.cpp.",
    "pieces: role -> {pack, file | files} under content/art/lot-3d/static (gitignored, licensed). A role missing here, or a file missing on disk, leaves the chunk mesh's own atlas face standing. files (a list) is one variant per file, picked by tile hash.",
    "width/height/thickness are the piece's own module in metres (1 m = 1 tile; a storey is 3). front is the local side that wears the outdoor finish (-z = the brick of the Generic walls; +z = a cornice or lamp that hangs on the outside). standoff is how far the piece's local z=0 sits proud of the face it dresses (walls: half their thickness). cutX/cutY are a door frame's jamb width and opening height, what the inside plaster is cut around.",
    "extent is a flat piece's local footprint [minX, minZ, maxX, maxZ], fitted to rectangles of whole tiles between minBlock and maxBlock on a side (a patterned piece wants 2..3 so its stones stay stones; a plank or a flat fill takes any rectangle). flipY mirrors a downward-facing quad upward (the plaster ceiling laid as a floor). upright stands a flat quad on its edge. twoSided draws a flat quad from both sides. lift is metres above the surface. tint multiplies the light (255 = the piece's own colours). selfLit keeps the tint whatever the light. maxDistance is the cheap cull in tiles.",
    "materials: a tile material id (content/raws/materials) -> wall class (masonry: brick out, plaster in; timber: the upright plank quad both sides; canvas: the kit wall's plaster both sides; none: chunk face), finish (brick, or plaster for a rendered stone building -- no brick corner piece on those), the outward tint, the indoor plaster tint (insideTint), the band below which its walls stay chunk, the floor piece its FLOOR cells wear (+ floorTint), the flat fill (+ fillTint) laid over the cells the floor piece could not fit, the lip quad on its slab sides (+ lipTint), and roof: true for a roofing material (its planes get the roof rules).",
    "minBand: nothing is placed below this band (the harbour's substrate). rules: one in how many -- windowEvery (pieces along an outdoor run, by index), chimneyEvery (roof-edge cells over masonry), propEvery (floor cells against one wall), tableEvery (the lattice pitch of the tables round an indoor lantern) / shelfEvery (indoor masonry face cells round one), roofTileEvery / roofPropEvery (roof cells / roof-edge cells), boatEvery (quay-edge cells over open water); hullFlareDegrees and hullTint dress a timber wall beside the harbour (a timber wall with a roofed room behind it is a building, never a hull); lanternFlame / fireFlame / litPane are the warm tints of a lamp's flame and a lit window at night; roofTint / roofFillTint are the roof finish every building top with sky over it wears, whatever its tile material: the dark fill (lead) with the roof_batten beam laid thin along every north-south module line, and the roof_flag piece only on a deck a stair or a ramp arrives on. floor_strip is the flag piece fitted to one cell (its stones as setts) on the one-wide cobble leftovers along a frontage. shop_sign hangs on a timber post beside a door gap; post_rail runs between a pair of lone posts two cells apart on a street at hip height. door_post is the strapped timber post, worn two ways: at its OWN section (width) it is a door's jamb, one on each wall cell flanking the opening, the ground to the head, with a joist laid across the two heads -- the chunk mesher leaves those two cells' boxes out under the head, so the beam is the jamb and not a decal on a metre-square block; and a lone timber cell out of doors with a job (within two cells of a door gap, or one of a pair that carries a rail) wears the same post FITTED to its cell (thickness is how far it stands proud of the cell), because that cell's box is still standing round it. A lone pier with no door and no partner keeps the pillar. The flame quad is one billboard turned to the eye.",
    "items (KIT BUILD): item id (content/raws/items/items.json) -> {pack, file, lift, pitchDegrees, yawOffsetDegrees, scale, tint, selfLit, maxDistance}: the static drawn on a tile for that thing, per frame off the room's hashed ground list (render3d/ground_items.cpp). A point-up weapon takes pitchDegrees 90 to lie flat. An id missing here draws nothing and changes nothing in the sim.",
    "THE LIGHT LAW (the panes after dark; render3d/static_pieces.cpp paneGlows(), read at relight in world_scene.cpp). A pane can glow once the sky's daylight is under paneDuskBelow (0.7 is last light, about ten to eight; the old 0.42 held every pane dark until twenty past). It glows by its HOUSEHOLD, never by itself: the roofed room behind the window is walked once and every window on that room draws one lot off the room's anchor cell with its storey folded out, so a house's windows agree, floor with floor where the footprints do -- the family is up or it is not. houseCandlePercent of households keep a candle at all; each puts it out at its own bedtime, hashed between houseBedtimeFrom and houseBedtimeTo (hours, past 24 for the small hours: 26 is two in the morning), and lights it again at its rising hour between houseRisingFrom and houseRisingTo, the early trades first; houseOwlPercent never put it out. So at eight the ward is up, by eleven a third have gone to bed, at two one house in six is still up, by three only the owls, and by five the fishers are stirring. litAllNight names the houses that keep their lights whatever the hour and keptDark the stores and yards kept dark but for a watchman's lamp in storeLampPercent of their windows (that one draws per pane, so a lamp is a window and never a whole warehouse) -- both by the sign's own place text (content/maps/src/docks_surface.tmx place_sign markers, native/include/granadad/sim/docks_signs_generated.hpp), read at the wall cell and then at the room cell behind it; a name no sign carries names nothing. A window with no roofed room behind it (a yard, a deck, a parapet, an open shed) is dark unless a lamp reaches the cell. pane_timber is the thin quad set in the hung timber window's frame, since that frame has no glass of its own: dark by day, warm by the same law, so a timber storey and a timber hovel can show a light at all."
  ],
